B.4 Reinforcement-Learning (GRPO) Setup
We sweep over β ∈ 10 -4 , , 10 -3 . Other GRPO settings are: ϵ lo = 0 . 20 , ϵ hi = 0 . 28 , ϵ adv = 10 -6 , batch size b = 1 , group size g = 2 , max new tokens 256 , T = 1 . 0 , topp = 1 . 0 . The optimizer is AdamW with η = 3 × 10 -5 , weight decay 0 . 01 , gradient clipping 1 . 0 , and bf16 precision [95]. The training data is capped to match the SFT data fractions { 20 , 40 , 60 , 80 , 100 } % of 20 , 000 examples. We include InterPro features in the prompt but exclude protein-protein interactions [74, 90]. The reward is computed against ontology-propagated leaf GO terms; examples without resolvable gold terms are dropped [89]. Both the GRPO reward and the evaluation metric are propagated unweighted GO-F1 [3, 89].
RL evaluation. For each trained run we evaluate on both the ID and OOD test splits. We select the checkpoint with the highest centred 50-step rolling mean of the training reward, as several runs at β = 10 -4 reach peak reward mid-training before drifting. Generation uses sampling at T = 0 . 7 with max new tokens 512 ; greedy decoding is avoided because the deterministic path on Qwen3-4B-Thinking tends to remain in the reasoning block without emitting a final answer.

--- CHUNK 60 ---
B Experimental Setup
B.5 LoRA Configurations and Trainable-Parameter Choices
All LoRA adapters target the seven attention and MLP projection matrices per transformer block: q, k, v, o, gate, up, down projections, with Gaussian initialization and no bias [93].
DNA LoRA. Both the DNA SFT and RL stages use r = 32 , α = 64 , dropout 0 . 05 on the same seven target modules. The Evo2 encoder is frozen; the DNA projection is trainable [38]. For RL, when the SFT and RL adapter ranks differ, we merge the SFT LoRA into the base weights and attach a fresh RL adapter at the requested rank [93].
RNA LoRA. The RNA SFT stage uses r = 64 , α = 64 , dropout 0 . 05 , while the RL stage uses r = 16 , α = 64 , dropout 0 . 05 on the same seven target modules. The transcriptformer encoder is frozen; the RNA projection is trainable [45]. For RL, when the SFT and RL adapter ranks differ, we merge the SFT LoRA into the base weights and attach a fresh RL adapter at the requested rank [93].
Protein SFT LoRA. Rank r = 128 , scaling α = 256 , dropout 0 . ESM-3 and the GO graph components are frozen; only the LoRA adapter on the text model and the protein-to-text projection layer receive gradients [52, 89, 93].
Protein RL warm-start. The SFT LoRA adapter and protein projection are extracted from the SFT checkpoint and reattached to the protein-conditioned Qwen policy used for GRPO [93, 5]. ESM-3 remains frozen [52]. During GRPO, the LoRA adapter and protein projection are trainable, while the underlying Qwen3-4B-Thinking weights are frozen [75]. The no-warm-start ablation trains the full 4 B-parameter model directly without a LoRA adapter.
